{"name":"Gin Still","hex":"#ba8e5a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Hinukh."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colourless non-aged alcoholic liquor made by distilling fermented grains such as barley, corn, oats or rye with juniper berries; the base for many cocktails.","Gin rummy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gin"},{"term":"still","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Not moving; calm.","Not effervescing; not sparkling."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Without motion.","Up to a time, as in the preceding time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/still"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gin","blocks":[{"text":"Gin is a distilled alcoholic drink flavoured with juniper berries and other botanical ingredients. It originated as a medicinal liquor made by monks and alchemists in the 12th Century in Salerno, Italy. The modern gin was modified in Flanders and the Netherlands to provide aqua vita from distillates of grapes and grains, becoming an object of commerce in the spirits industry. Gin became popular in England after the introduction of jenever, a Dutch and Belgian liquor. Although this development had been taking place since the early 17th century, gin became widespread after the 1688 Glorious Revolution led by William of Orange and subsequent import restrictions on French brandy. Gin emerged as the national alcoholic drink of England during the Gin Craze of 1695–1735."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/DecaturGins.jpg/330px-DecaturGins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gin","wikidata":"Q959362"},{"title":"Still","blocks":[{"text":"A still is an apparatus used to distill liquid mixtures by heating to selectively boil and then cooling to condense the vapor. A still uses the same concepts as a basic distillation apparatus, but on a much larger scale. Stills have been used to produce perfume and medicine, water for injection (WFI) for pharmaceutical use, generally to separate and purify different chemicals, and to produce distilled beverages containing ethanol."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Glenfiddich_Distillery_stills.jpg/330px-Glenfiddich_Distillery_stills.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Still","wikidata":"Q1436130"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg/500px-William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg/1280px-William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg/500px-William_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hogarth's Gin Lane (created 1750–1751)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliam_Hogarth_-_Gin_Lane.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg/500px-The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg/1280px-The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_-_BL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/The_Gin_shop_-_Cruikshank%2C_Scraps_and_sketches_%281829%29%2C_f.9_